afe destination for  tourists but this perceived normality  didn't just come by itself strict  restrictions and mask mandates kept  infection rates low it's a pandemic  success story that worked well  [Music]  with the longest slide measuring 450  meters and the steepest of them enabling  speeds of up to 70 kilometers per hour  the aqua venture park in dubai is quite  the tourist attraction  usually it welcomes 6 000 visitors a day  from all over the world  but when the pandemic hit the park's  future was thrown into doubt  zachar trimer who is in charge of its  800 employees was worried  but the united arab emirates which dubai  is a part of quickly came up with a plan  implement tight coronavirus restrictions  to get public life going again and  tourism too it's one of the country's  most important sources of income  people here had to wear masks both  indoors and outdoors you could get a pcr  test on every corner and covert vaccines  became available for everyone in early  2021  so people really didn't talk about it  restrictions were simply imposed you had  to get vaccinated but it was free of  charge  and i think dubai or the uae had a  relatively fast and high vaccination  rate over 90 percent it was then of  course also really good for tourism and  i think it helped bring tourism back to  the region relatively quickly  the so-called dubai model worked the uae  was able to keep infection numbers low  by mid-2021 when many countries had  closed their borders dubai was once  again a sought after destination  it was helped by hotels like the  atlantis meticulously following the  regulations with more than 1 500 rooms  the atlantis is almost a small town the  water park is part of it  jurg unger is in charge of the hotel's  23 restaurants and 500 cooks  for months here staff had to monitor the  guests in the restaurants and make sure  they followed social distancing mask  wearing and disinfection rules  it was an additional burden for the  employees  inside as well as out the requirements  to wear masks outdoors was dropped only  a few weeks ago  i think it's hard  i think it was worth it it was the right  strategy to approach this with strict  rules and also to be very strict with  distancing rules we're almost able to  totally forget about it  nevertheless people here are anything  but flippant about the pandemic  perhaps precisely because dubai fared so  much better than other places  islam kasim is in charge of tourism  strategy for the uae he says people  remain cautious because they recognized  how quickly the covet strategy brought  back a sense of normalcy  so if you're walking in a mall and  you've forgotten your mask by your chin  the the resident or the guest next to  you would look at you say can you please  put the mask up and i think that kind of  brought the uh the the pandemic to more  of uh of of the mainstream of people to  realize that in order for them to be  able to go about their life in a normal  way  they need to respect these regulations  the fact that we were able  to push through and come out the way  that we did on the other side shows you  that we are ready for for such  challenges to come up in the future  the pandemic may hardly be noticeable  for many vacationers at the atlantis  there's still a mask requirement indoors  though  germans jerk unga and zasha trima  sometimes hear about protests against  coronavirus measures back home in the  news  and about people who are reluctant to  get vaccinated  they think things went better here  because of transparent communication  about restrictions mandatory testing and  vaccinations  and because many people's jobs are  connected to tourism  you can understand the people you can  also understand their fears  for us the fears here are different  they're more about if guests don't come  at all  so that's our fear  because that's what we live on  if you talk to people who work in hotels  they often tell you the coronavirus is  taking a break but the pand